This is a very big upgrade as this is where we will see both the code work simultaneoulsy complementing each other at the basic level. The grid is formed intially with randomly distributed cells. I first made two independent codes- one for the discrete heat diffusion equation solver, another for CA analysis that has been made until now. These two were later combined in a singular code and then tweaked accordingly to reflect the true physics. 
The heat source inputs heat into the grid. The thermal solver will solve the heat diffusion equation and then calculate the thermal gradients and the cooling rate formed on the basis of the heat source motion.

-------------------------------------------------------------

The initial changes were
-to have a smaller grain size (more number of nuclie : increased from 20  to 1600)
-to have a gaussian heat source that moves on a straight line with a specified heat input radius and decay area
-incorporate the aspects of cooling_coefficient to represent the convective losses indirectly; density, latent heat, thermal diffusivity and cp of the material to model a sensible heat input; effects of ambient temperatures and constant wall temperatures which act heat sinks; and other physical properties. 

The flow of the code is as the first part forms a random distribution of grains in our observation window. The heat source is then given to the sample which raises the temperature and causes the melting to begin. Meanwhile the motion of heat source is also carried out. In the same timestep, the heat diffusion solver calculates the new temperature gradients and updates the thermal profile for the next timestep to solve for grain growth. The CA part then governs the grain growth based on the current thermal situation which is updated in every timestep. 


The current model completely lacks the inclusion of thermal stresses and fluid motion in the molten weld pool, as well as dimensional relevance to reality. Appropriate scaling was done by hit and trial and with references of guides. 



Features of solver in V1--

moving weld heat source,
transient heat diffusion,
melt pool formation,
remelting,
epitaxial regrowth,
stochastic capture,
dendritic anisotropy,
thermal-gradient-driven growth,
competitive columnar growth,
randomized CA updates

-----------------------
Note-
Transient heat diffusion had to solve for the probability based on angle of the grain compared to the thermal gradient which was formulated using cosine function. However, since cosine hits 0 at an angle, perpendicular directions were completely prohibited from grain growth. To prevent this, the function was changed such that it always stays strictly positive. This gave rise to a different directional preference once solidification occurs, that only the top(or bottom) cells were solidifying upon passage of the heat source depending on the sign of the thermal gradient. This was eventually settled by taking absolute (mod) of the cosine value in the first place since the negative parts and positive parts will contribute equally to the probability of regrowth. This allowed for a propoer teardrop shaped weld pool to form and traverse across the sample. 
The next issue for the selection of grains to regrow was also having an indirect bias because of the existense of the for loop. Since it always gave grains falling in the initial checking sequence more chance and hindered natural formation behaviour. This was eventually fixed by randomising the grain selection order as well from a list of cells to be checked and picked randomly. 
-----------------------


